  "tour": {
    "title": "Romans Before You Read: Paul’s Argument, Audience, and Four-Part Shape",
    "intro": "Before you begin Romans, this tour will give you a map of the letter without reducing it to a list of doctrines. You’ll first hear about the Roman churches and the tensions Paul is addressing, then get a unifying lens for his theology, and finally walk through the letter’s four large movements from Romans 1 to 16.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:63bf2f5f-com-6cd6a9f3-108c-38ad-b4b8-02550ba51633",
        "startSec": 653,
        "endSec": 1004,
        "why": "Begin with the situation behind the letter. The teacher explains that Paul is introducing his ministry and preparing Rome to support his mission westward, while addressing a network of house churches divided between Jewish and Gentile believers. Listen for the “fairly large Gentile component” that “tends to look down on the Jews,” and for Paul’s goal that they worship “with one mind and one voice.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Backstory to Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/i5pj3w/backstory.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1197,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:powerful-and-not-powerful",
        "startSec": 3160,
        "endSec": 3422,
        "why": "This supplies the lens to carry into the rest of the tour: the speakers say Paul’s “dream is for a unified, multi-ethnic family of God,” and connect the theology of Romans 1–11 to that dream. Their striking summary is that “justification by faith” is, for Paul, a “theological resource for how to achieve unity,” not merely an abstract answer to an individual question.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Powerful and Not Powerful",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/305c0968-3057-456e-af97-d810bae76ba9/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=305c0968-3057-456e-af97-d810bae76ba9",
        "durationSec": 3952,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 80,
        "endSec": 390,
        "why": "Now hear the first half of the letter as a structural map. The teacher divides Romans into four parts and identifies 1:16–17 as its thesis: “the whole rest of the letter” unfolds that sentence. The clip then traces chapters 1–4—from Gentile, moral, and Jewish hearers all standing under condemnation to God’s righteousness through Jesus and Abraham—and chapters 5–8, where faith, Christ, and the Spirit address sin, death, and condemnation.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 390,
        "endSec": 869,
        "why": "Finish the map by hearing why chapters 9–16 are not an interruption. The teacher frames 9–11 around the question, “what about all the Jews who haven't believed?” and describes God’s one family tree of Jews and Gentiles. Then chapters 12–16 become the “therefore”: concrete practices of welcoming one another, handling food and festival disagreements, pursuing one voice in worship, and finally the greetings and house churches of chapter 16.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "As you read, keep two questions beside the text: how does each section advance Paul’s claim about God’s righteousness revealed in the gospel, and how does it move Jews and Gentiles toward one family? Romans moves from the universal problem, through God’s action in Christ and the Spirit and his faithfulness to Israel, into the hard work of a reconciled community.",
    "totalSeconds": 1402
  },
